     "text": "**GATE Exam Master Trap: Can $X(s) = \\frac{\\sinh s}{D(s)}$ ever be Causal or Anti-Causal?**\nExpand the hyperbolic sine:\n$$\\sinh s = \\frac{e^s - e^{-s}}{2} \\implies X(s) = \\frac{e^s}{2 D(s)} - \\frac{e^{-s}}{2 D(s)}$$\nLet $X_0(s) = \\frac{1}{2 D(s)} \\longleftrightarrow x_0(t)$. Then $x(t) = x_0(t + 1) - x_0(t - 1)$.\n- **If ROC is rightmost (all poles right-sided):**\n  $x_0(t)$ is causal ($u(t)$). Then $x_0(t+1)$ has factor $u(t+1)$, which is non-zero for $t \\in [-1, 0)$. Thus $x(t) \\ne 0$ for $t < 0 \\implies \\mathbf{NON\\text{-}CAUSAL}$.\n- **If ROC is leftmost (all poles left-sided):**\n  $x_0(t)$ is anti-causal ($u(-t)$). Then $x_0(t-1)$ has factor $u(-(t-1)) = u(-t+1)$, which is non-zero for $t \\in (0, 1]$. Thus $x(t) \\ne 0$ for $t > 0 \\implies \\mathbf{NON\\text{-}CAUSAL}$.\n- **If ROC is an intermediate strip:**\n  A mixture of right-sided and left-sided terms appears, both shifted, producing non-zero support across both positive and negative time $\\implies \\mathbf{NON\\text{-}CAUSAL}$.\n\n**Universal Conclusion:** For **ANY** valid ROC strip, $x(t)$ can **NEVER** be causal and can **NEVER** be anti-causal. **$x(t)$ IS UNIQUELY AND STRICTLY NON-CAUSAL!**"
